# Reviewed 2026-08-20. Every entry below is CRITICAL/HIGH, has an upstream fix,
# and cannot be resolved without breaking a pin this image exists to hold.
# Anything not listed here is genuinely fixed, not suppressed -- the previous
# ~107-entry list was almost entirely Go stdlib noise from binaries built with
# old toolchains, and that is now dealt with by compiling from source.
#
# Re-check this file whenever kubectl or helmfile move.

# --- kubectl: pinned to 1.21.14 for the ACP clusters ------------------------
# Server-side Kubernetes vulnerabilities (kubelet, CSI, admission, node
# handling). kubectl is a client binary and does not run any of this code, but
# trivy flags them because the k8s.io/kubernetes module is in its graph.
# The only fix upstream offers is a Kubernetes upgrade, which the 1.21.14 pin
# explicitly forbids. Dependency CVEs in kubectl are fixed, not ignored --
# see GO_SECURITY_BUMPS in the Dockerfile.
CVE-2023-3676
CVE-2023-3955
CVE-2023-5528
CVE-2024-0793
CVE-2024-10220
CVE-2024-5321
# docker/distribution v2.7.1, reachable only from kubectl's unused image code.
CVE-2023-2253
# golang.org/x/oauth2: bumping it pulls a cloud.google.com/go that collides
# with the version k8s 1.21 pins ("ambiguous import"). Costs one finding.
CVE-2025-22868

# --- helmfile 0.171.0 -------------------------------------------------------
# 0.171.0 is the last 0.x release; the line ended and v1 took over. Its
# dependency graph rejects a grpc bump outright ("ambiguous import" between
# grpc's own stats/opentelemetry and the standalone module of that path), and
# the rest below are pinned transitively behind it. All are fixed by moving to
# helmfile v1.x, which is a breaking change for consumers' helmfile.yaml and
# so is a deliberate decision for another day, not a silent upgrade here.
CVE-2026-33186
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf
CVE-2025-8959
CVE-2026-4660
CVE-2026-24051
CVE-2026-39883
CVE-2026-34986
CVE-2025-30204
CVE-2025-53547
CVE-2025-15558
CVE-2026-34040
CVE-2026-32287
